            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has alot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the three doors, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). 

As you are conducting a fairly large renovation to the exterior walls, with attention to detail, you should expect an improvement on the air leakage. 



Air leakage was also observed through all three bathroom exhaust fans (this includes the house fan in the spare bedroom). I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 20% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 600 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding an additional 2 inches (R12) rigid foam, polyisocyanerate, insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding another R12 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 420&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 10 inches (R32) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled an additional 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</ceilings>
                <HotWaterSystem>

As per our discussion of potentially switching from an oil to propane, I have modelled replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Savings are based on national average hot water use.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Furnace&lt;/b&gt;

As per our discussion of potentially switching from an oil to propane, I have modelled replacing your current oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;21.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Due to recent oil prices, savings are minimal when switching to propane, however historically propane has been a cheaper source of fuel. 





&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current pellet stove for 75% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 400&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



If you are considering replacing your current pellet stove, I recommend you install a pellet stove that is EPA Certified. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air supply. 

By installing an EPA Certified pellet stove, you could potentially qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.





&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

As per our discussion of potentially switching from an oil to propane, I have modelled replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Savings are based on national average hot water use.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



